 sufilinde many have  tried and failed to recreate these  delicately exquisite noodles for those  who want a bang in each bite taiwan is  the place to go only on moonless nights  fishermen head out into the pacific to  fire fish  that's right  they set off explosives in the water  which creates a light attracting  thousands of sardines but why go fishing  when the fish can come to you 124 miles  from the ocean is euro honduras its  first major rainfall of the spring  brings thunder  lightning and fish  the juvia depeches or rain of fish fills  the streets with the still living  creatures fresh fish for the taking to  toast our journey around the world  champagne  bet you didn't know that this used to be  considered  a performance enhancer  they even guzzled the bubbly during a  marathon at the 1908 london olympics  [Music]  i won't be doing that because we know  better and also because i'm probably  going to have another  tough assignment i know dylan thirus the  co-founder of atlas obscura and the  co-author of gastro spirit joins us now  welcome good morning  so let's talk about this atlas obscurate  and the team how did you find all these  stories because you go all over the  world yeah so so both atlas obscura and  gastro obscura work in the same way  which is we started both  as a way of telling incredible hidden  stories around the world sort of unsung  wonders and in both cases we reached out  to the community and they said tell us  about the most interesting stories near  you in this case it's all about what are  the most interesting food stories drink  stories  restaurants and so that's how it started  this has been a book four years in the  making and so people sent in stuff and  then my co-author cece and i so half the  entries come from there and then about  half come from cecily and i exploring  the world and finding things to add can  i just say something about unsung  wonders like lime cured bull testicles  thank you  big ass ants peru no thank you stuffed  camel spleen and we want to taste that  wide here let me explain let me let me  explain something so i i i don't know  where all of you are from but  i grew up in minnesota  i grew up in minnesota eating uh  lutefisk i'm scandinavian so lutefisk is  like dried fish that's then soaked in  lie there's a period where it's  poisonous it would murder you if you ate  it but they wash out the lie and then  you eat it and this is extremely popular  in the upper midwest and sort of  scandinavian areas  the point i'm saying is that basically  what is unusual what is strange is fully  relative to where you're from so like we  as americans eat an enormous amount of  cheese much of the world finds this  extremely disturbing and they smell like  cheese  and so this is kind of the point is that  it's all from where you stand what is  interesting what is unusual what is  different so gail show the book because  it's a gorgeous book yeah it's beautiful  love it i guess it's beautifully done  what you've got mark there is what  you're going to try or what you will  never try  a little bit of both yeah a lot of tabs  right there it's a lot of times but  you've got a beautiful picture of a  wedding bread a wedding bread that i  thought was one of the prettiest things  i've ever seen ukrainian wedding bread  yeah beautiful intricate stamped with  this special uh kind of a form yeah you  want you want the best looking bread  possible on your wedding of course so  dylan how do you how would you say  people should use that book because it's  kind of big for walking around on a  backpack it's not necessarily a little  travel i think there's two ways one is  as a kind of  piece of armchair travel so you can open  this book to anywhere any page and just  step into another little piece of the  world and get a sense of their food  traditions their  special flavors the things that are  really local to that place and then it's  also great to check it when you're  traveling and see maybe there's  something i want to try while i'm here  yeah it immediately became one of our  favorite books my wife and i we travel  because of food and culture so that's  perfect um what story left an imprint on  you a little bit i'll do a little  something all right and i'll try some of  that stuff  now try it once testicles you're ready  yeah yeah this is supposed to be an  appetizer so go slow yeah  they're going to take it easy  you guys love poetry  so what story left an imprint on you out  of all of these you know what's  interesting there are the ones that are  obviously surprising and then the ones  that you're familiar with but you don't  know the backstory of so in this book  actually we have an entry on pad thai  and no one is going to be we've all  eaten pad thai almost certainly but one  of the things people might not realize  is that it was kind of an invented dish  it was in the 30s the the then kind of  ruler of thailand wanted to create a new  state and said okay we need a national  dish he basically invented pad thai  because it was something he grew up  eating at home and said this is now  going to be dylan the national dish for  thailand i'm telling we have to go to  the berries okay yes we should do it  this this is all right great this is  this is the one i had in the green one  okay don't chew these there's no  okay  you just just watch out not to eat so  how are you supposed to eat them if  you're not supposed to cheese it no you  chew the berry but just watch out for  the seed in the middle oh or the scene  sour things sweet yes these are from  west africa and they're used people eat  them before drinking sour wine or eating  sour bread and they'll turn these lemons  into kind of almost like a candy tasting  treat so that's okay don't buy them  they're called miracle berries they're  called miracle berries and they're  miracle berries yeah i love them i'm not  going to try the lemon i've never had  these okay these are try a little lemon  with thi